  S.speaking.push(
    { id: 'tsw-op5', task: 'opinion', title: 'Open-Plan Offices', accent: 'en-GB', prep: 45, secs: 60,
      q: 'Do you agree or disagree with the following statement? Open-plan offices, where many employees work in one large room, are better than private offices. Give specific reasons and examples.',
      model: "I mostly disagree with this statement. Open-plan offices do make it easier for colleagues to share information quickly, and they can feel more friendly. However, I think the disadvantages are greater. The main problem is noise. When people around you are on the phone or chatting, it's very hard to concentrate on detailed work. For example, at my last job, I often stayed late just to finish reports when the office was quiet. Another issue is privacy, since some conversations, such as with clients or about personal matters, shouldn't be overheard. In my opinion, the best solution is a mix: shared areas for teamwork and small quiet rooms that people can book when they need to focus.",
      mid: "I disagree. Open-plan offices are very noisy. People talk on the phone and it is hard to work. Also there is no privacy. I prefer private offices because they are quiet.",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình nêu đúng hai nhược điểm nhưng chỉ khoảng 20 giây, không giải thích, không ví dụ; không nhắc mặt lợi nên thiếu cân nhắc. Bài mẫu thừa nhận ưu điểm, phát triển hai lý do có ví dụ và đề xuất mô hình kết hợp.',
      outline: ['Phần lớn không đồng ý', 'Thừa nhận ưu điểm: trao đổi nhanh, thân thiện', 'Lý do 1: tiếng ồn + ví dụ ở lại muộn', 'Lý do 2: quyền riêng tư', 'Giải pháp kết hợp'],
      checklist: CK_OP },
    { id: 'tsw-op6', task: 'opinion', title: 'Meetings in Person or Online?', accent: 'en-US', prep: 45, secs: 60,
      q: 'Which do you think is more effective: meetings held in person or meetings held online? Give specific reasons and examples to support your opinion.',
      model: "I think in-person meetings are more effective for important discussions, although online meetings are useful for quick updates. When people are in the same room, it's easier to read body language and to have a natural conversation, so misunderstandings can be cleared up quickly. For example, my team once spent three online calls trying to agree on a project plan, but we solved it in one face-to-face meeting. On the other hand, online meetings save travel time and work well when the purpose is simply to share information. So, in my view, companies should use online meetings for routine updates but meet in person when decisions or creative ideas are needed.",
      mid: "I think online meetings are better. You don't need to travel and you can join from home. It saves time and money. Also you can record the meeting. So online meetings are more effective.",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình có các lợi ích đúng nhưng chỉ liệt kê, không ví dụ, khoảng 20 giây; "effective" được hiểu là "tiện lợi", chưa bàn đến chất lượng thảo luận. Bài mẫu phân biệt loại cuộc họp, có ví dụ cụ thể và kết luận cân bằng.',
      outline: ['Trực tiếp hiệu quả hơn với thảo luận quan trọng', 'Lý do: ngôn ngữ cơ thể, giải quyết hiểu lầm nhanh + ví dụ 3 cuộc gọi', 'Nhượng bộ: online tiết kiệm thời gian, hợp cập nhật', 'Kết luận: dùng tùy mục đích'],
      checklist: CK_OP },
    { id: 'tsw-op7', task: 'opinion', title: 'Retirement Age', accent: 'en-GB', prep: 45, secs: 60,
      q: 'Some people believe that workers should be allowed to keep working for as long as they want, instead of retiring at a fixed age. Do you agree or disagree? Give specific reasons and examples.',
      model: "I agree that people should be able to choose when they retire. First, many people stay healthy and productive well into their sixties and seventies, and forcing them to stop wastes their experience. For example, my grandfather worked as an engineer until he was seventy, and younger colleagues often asked him for advice on difficult projects. Second, some people simply enjoy their work, and having a purpose every day can be good for their mental health. Of course, companies should still be able to discuss performance with older workers, just as they do with everyone else. But age alone shouldn't be the reason someone has to leave a job they're good at.",
      mid: "I agree. Old people have a lot of experience. If they are healthy they can work. Some old people need money too. So they should work as long as they want.",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình có hai lý do hợp lý nhưng không phát triển, không ví dụ, quá ngắn; "Old people" nghe thiếu tế nhị (nên dùng older workers). Bài mẫu có ví dụ người ông kỹ sư, lý do về sức khỏe tinh thần và một điều kiện công bằng.',
      outline: ['Đồng ý', 'Lý do 1: còn khỏe, giàu kinh nghiệm + ví dụ người ông', 'Lý do 2: niềm vui, mục đích sống', 'Điều kiện: đánh giá hiệu quả như mọi người', 'Kết luận'],
      checklist: CK_OP },
    { id: 'tsw-op8', task: 'opinion', title: 'Customer Reviews and Business', accent: 'en-US', prep: 45, secs: 60,
      q: 'Do you agree or disagree with the following statement? Online customer reviews are the most important factor for the success of a small business today. Give specific reasons and examples.',
      model: "I agree that online reviews are extremely important, but I don't think they're the most important factor. It's true that most customers check reviews before visiting a new restaurant or shop, so a few bad reviews can really hurt a small business. However, reviews are only a result of something more basic: the quality of the product and the service. For example, a bakery near my home has excellent reviews, but that's because the owner uses good ingredients and remembers regular customers' names. If the quality were poor, no marketing could fix the reviews for long. So, in my opinion, quality and customer service come first, and good reviews follow naturally.",
      mid: "I agree. Today everyone reads reviews on the internet. If a business has bad reviews, people don't go there. So reviews are very important for small businesses.",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình đồng ý nhưng chỉ nhắc lại đề bằng lời khác, không ví dụ, khoảng 15 giây. Bài mẫu đặt câu hỏi về chữ "most important", lập luận rằng đánh giá là kết quả của chất lượng, có ví dụ tiệm bánh cụ thể.',
      outline: ['Quan trọng nhưng không phải quan trọng nhất', 'Thừa nhận: khách đọc đánh giá trước khi đến', 'Lập luận: đánh giá là kết quả của chất lượng + ví dụ tiệm bánh', 'Kết luận: chất lượng và dịch vụ trước'],
      checklist: CK_OP },
  );
  S.writing.push(
    { id: 'tsw-wr4', task: 'request', title: 'Conference Registration', minutes: 10, maxScore: 4,
      email: { from: 'Registration Team, National Retail Conference', to: 'You', subject: 'Your conference registration',
        body: "Thank you for registering for the National Retail Conference on November 3–4. We are now finalizing the program and catering. Please reply with any questions you have and let us know if you have any special requirements." },
      directions: 'Respond to the e-mail as if you are a conference participant. In your e-mail, ask TWO questions and make ONE request.',
      model: "Dear Registration Team,\n\nThank you for confirming my registration for the National Retail Conference.\n\nI have two questions. First, will the presentation slides be available to participants after the conference? Several sessions take place at the same time, and I would like to review the ones I cannot attend. Second, is there a discounted rate at any hotels near the venue for conference participants?\n\nI also have one request. I am vegetarian, so I would be grateful if you could arrange vegetarian options for me at lunch on both days.\n\nThank you for your help, and I look forward to the event.\n\nBest regards,\nAngela Ruiz",
      mid: "Hi,\n\nI have some questions. Can I get the slides? Is there a hotel near? Also I am vegetarian.\n\nThanks,\nAngela",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình có 2 câu hỏi nhưng "Is there a hotel near?" thiếu tân ngữ (→ near the venue); "I am vegetarian" chỉ là thông tin, không phải yêu cầu – thiếu nhiệm vụ thứ ba; giọng quá ngắn gọn với ban tổ chức → khoảng 2/4. Bài mẫu đánh số rõ ràng, có lý do cho từng câu hỏi và yêu cầu cụ thể.',
      checklist: CK_REQ },
    { id: 'tsw-wr5', task: 'request', title: 'New Employee Equipment', minutes: 10, maxScore: 4,
      email: { from: 'Jason Park, IT Department', to: 'New employees', subject: 'Equipment for your first day',
        body: "Welcome to the company! Before you start next Monday, we would like to prepare your computer and other equipment. Please let us know about any equipment or software you will need for your work." },
      directions: 'Respond to the e-mail as if you are a new employee. In your e-mail, make TWO requests and give ONE piece of information.',
      model: "Dear Mr. Park,\n\nThank you for your email. I am joining the marketing team as a graphic designer next Monday, and I appreciate your help in preparing my equipment.\n\nI have two requests. First, could my computer have the latest version of the design software we use, as I will need it for most of my projects? Second, would it be possible to provide a larger monitor, since I often work on detailed images?\n\nI would also like to let you know that I will be working from home on Fridays, as agreed with my manager. I would therefore need access to the company network from my laptop.\n\nThank you in advance.\n\nKind regards,\nYuki Tanaka",
      mid: "Dear Jason,\n\nI need design software and a big monitor. I work from home on Friday.\n\nThank you,\nYuki",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình làm đủ nhiệm vụ về mặt hình thức nhưng chỉ liệt kê, không giới thiệu bản thân, không giải thích lý do, giọng ra lệnh ("I need…") → khoảng 2/4. Bài mẫu giới thiệu vị trí công việc, dùng "could…/would it be possible…" và giải thích từng yêu cầu.',
      checklist: CK_REQ },
    { id: 'tsw-wr6', task: 'request', title: 'Feedback on a Hotel Stay', minutes: 10, maxScore: 4,
      email: { from: 'Guest Services, Harbor View Hotel', to: 'You', subject: 'How was your stay?',
        body: "Thank you for staying at the Harbor View Hotel last weekend. We hope you enjoyed your visit. We would be grateful if you could share your comments so that we can continue to improve our service." },
      directions: 'Respond to the e-mail as if you were a guest at the hotel. In your e-mail, describe ONE thing you liked, ONE problem you had and make ONE suggestion.',
      model: "Dear Guest Services Team,\n\nThank you for your email. Overall, I enjoyed my stay at the Harbor View Hotel.\n\nWhat I liked most was the staff at the front desk. When I arrived late on Friday night, the receptionist was very welcoming and even recommended a restaurant that was still open nearby.\n\nHowever, I did have one problem. The air conditioning in my room, number 512, was very noisy, and I found it difficult to sleep. I mentioned it on Saturday morning, but it had not been repaired by the evening.\n\nI would suggest checking the air conditioning units regularly, and perhaps offering guests a different room when a repair cannot be done quickly.\n\nBest regards,\nMarcus Hill",
      mid: "Hello,\n\nThe staff was nice. But the air conditioner was noisy and I didn't sleep well. You should fix it.\n\nMarcus",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình đủ 3 ý nhưng mỗi ý rất sơ sài ("The staff was nice"), góp ý "You should fix it" quá thẳng và chung chung; thiếu số phòng, thời điểm → khoảng 2/4. Bài mẫu có chi tiết cụ thể cho cả lời khen, vấn đề (số phòng, đã báo nhưng chưa sửa) và góp ý khả thi.',
      checklist: CK_REQ },
  );
  S.writing.push(
    { id: 'tsw-es4', task: 'essay', title: 'Technology and Working Hours', minutes: 30, maxScore: 5,
      q: 'Some people believe that technology such as smartphones and laptops has made employees work longer hours. Others think technology has made work more efficient and given people more free time. Which view do you agree with? Give reasons or examples to support your opinion.',
      model: "Over the past twenty years, smartphones and laptops have transformed the way people work. While these tools have certainly made many tasks faster, I believe that for most office workers they have actually increased working hours rather than reduced them.\n\nThe main reason is that technology has removed the clear boundary between work and personal life. In the past, when employees left the office, their work stayed there. Today, work emails and messages arrive on our phones in the evening, at the weekend and even on holiday. Many people feel that they must reply quickly to show that they are committed, so they continue working long after their official hours have ended. A friend of mine who works in sales told me that she spends about an hour every evening answering messages from clients, which adds up to more than five extra hours a week.\n\nSecond, the efficiency that technology creates is often used to increase workloads rather than to give people more free time. If a report that once took a day can now be finished in two hours, managers usually expect employees to produce more reports, not to go home early. As a result, the benefits of faster work go mainly to the company, while employees remain just as busy as before.\n\nOf course, technology does have advantages. It allows some people to work from home, which saves commuting time, and it makes communication with colleagues in other countries much easier. However, these benefits only lead to more free time when companies set clear rules, such as not sending emails after a certain hour.\n\nIn conclusion, although technology has the potential to make work more efficient, in practice it has encouraged many employees to work longer hours. Companies and workers need to set limits if technology is to improve people's lives.",
      mid: "Today technology is everywhere in our work. Some people think it makes us work more and some people think it gives us more free time. I think technology makes us work more.\n\nFirst, we can check emails on our phone at any time. So we work at home in the evening and at weekends. This is not good for our health.\n\nSecond, the boss can contact us easily. If the boss sends a message at night, we have to answer.\n\nBut technology also has good points. We can work faster and we can work from home.\n\nIn conclusion, I think technology makes people work longer hours, so it is not good.",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình (khoảng 115 từ) có quan điểm và hai ý đúng nhưng quá ngắn so với 300 từ; ý phát triển chỉ 1–2 câu; không có ví dụ cụ thể; kết luận "so it is not good" khái quát hóa. Thang ETS khoảng 3/5. Bài mẫu có hai lập luận sâu (mất ranh giới, hiệu quả bị dùng để tăng khối lượng việc), ví dụ có số liệu, và nhượng bộ có điều kiện.',
      outline: ['Mở bài: công nghệ thay đổi cách làm việc + quan điểm (tăng giờ làm)', 'Thân 1: mất ranh giới công việc – đời sống + ví dụ người bạn bán hàng', 'Thân 2: hiệu quả bị dùng để tăng khối lượng việc', 'Nhượng bộ: làm từ xa, giao tiếp – chỉ có lợi khi có quy định', 'Kết bài'],
      checklist: CK_ESSAY },
    { id: 'tsw-es5', task: 'essay', title: 'Experience or Education?', minutes: 30, maxScore: 5,
      q: 'When hiring new employees, some companies value work experience more than a university degree. Do you agree that work experience is more important than education? Give reasons or examples to support your opinion.',
      model: "Employers often have to choose between candidates with strong academic qualifications and those with practical experience. Although a degree is valuable, I agree that in most jobs work experience is the more important factor.\n\nThe first reason is that experience shows how a person actually performs in a real workplace. A degree proves that someone can study, pass exams and understand theory, but it does not show whether they can meet deadlines, deal with difficult customers or work well in a team. Someone who has held a job for several years has already demonstrated these abilities. For example, when my uncle hired an assistant for his accounting firm, he chose a candidate with three years of experience in a small office over a recent graduate with excellent grades, because she already knew how to manage clients' records and could start working immediately.\n\nIn addition, experienced employees usually need less training, which saves companies both time and money. New graduates often need months to adapt to office routines, learn company software and understand how decisions are made. During this period, other staff must spend time supervising them. An experienced employee, by contrast, can contribute much sooner and may even bring useful ideas from previous jobs. This is particularly valuable for small companies, which cannot afford to spend months preparing a new member of staff.\n\nNevertheless, education remains essential in certain fields. Doctors, engineers and lawyers must have formal qualifications, and in these professions experience cannot replace specialized knowledge. Moreover, a degree can be a good indicator of discipline and the ability to learn, which matter for young applicants who have not had the chance to gain experience.\n\nIn conclusion, while a university degree is necessary in some professions, work experience is generally more important because it proves a candidate's practical abilities and reduces the cost of training.",
      mid: "Some companies think experience is more important than a degree. I agree with this idea.\n\nFirst, people with experience know how to do the job. They don't need training. For example, a person who worked in a shop for five years knows how to talk to customers.\n\nSecond, a degree is only paper. Many students study theory at university but they cannot use it in real work.\n\nHowever, some jobs need a degree, like doctor.\n\nIn conclusion, experience is more important than education for most jobs.",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình (khoảng 100 từ) có cấu trúc đúng nhưng quá ngắn; "a degree is only paper" khái quát hóa thiếu công bằng; nhượng bộ chỉ một câu và sai mạo từ ("like doctor" → like doctors). Thang ETS khoảng 3/5. Bài mẫu phát triển hai lý do có ví dụ cụ thể và nhượng bộ đầy đủ về các ngành cần bằng cấp.',
      outline: ['Mở bài: đồng ý – kinh nghiệm quan trọng hơn ở đa số công việc', 'Thân 1: kinh nghiệm chứng minh năng lực thực tế + ví dụ người chú tuyển trợ lý', 'Thân 2: ít phải đào tạo, đóng góp sớm', 'Nhượng bộ: ngành cần bằng cấp; bằng cấp phản ánh kỷ luật', 'Kết bài'],
      checklist: CK_ESSAY },
    { id: 'tsw-es6', task: 'essay', title: 'Shopping Locally or Online', minutes: 30, maxScore: 5,
      q: 'Some people prefer to buy products from small local shops, while others prefer large online retailers. Which do you prefer? Give reasons or examples to support your opinion.',
      model: "With online shopping becoming faster and cheaper every year, many people now buy almost everything from large internet retailers. However, whenever possible, I prefer to buy from small local shops, because they offer better service and help to keep my community lively.\n\nThe main reason for my preference is the personal service that local shops provide. Owners and staff of small shops usually know their products very well and can give advice that no website can offer. For instance, when I wanted to buy a pair of running shoes, the owner of a small sports shop near my home watched me run for a few minutes and recommended a model that suited the way I move. Since then, I have had no knee pain, which had been a problem with shoes I had bought online without trying them.\n\nAnother reason is the effect on the local community. When people spend money in neighbourhood shops, a larger share of that money stays in the area, paying the wages of local workers and supporting other local businesses. Streets with independent shops are also more pleasant places to live, because they bring people outside and create opportunities to meet neighbours. In contrast, when local shops close because everyone shops online, high streets can become empty and unsafe.\n\nI admit that online retailers have clear advantages. They often offer lower prices and a wider choice, and they are very convenient for busy people or for those who live far from shops. For items such as electronics or books, I sometimes buy online myself.\n\nNevertheless, for most everyday purchases, the expert advice and the benefits to my community make local shops my first choice. In my opinion, a small amount of extra cost is a fair price for a more personal service and a livelier neighbourhood.",
      mid: "I prefer small local shops. There are two reasons.\n\nFirst, the staff in local shops are friendly and they can help you. On the internet nobody helps you.\n\nSecond, if we buy from local shops, we help our community. If everyone buys online, the small shops will close.\n\nBut online shopping is cheaper and more convenient. Sometimes I buy online too.\n\nIn conclusion, I prefer local shops because of the friendly staff and the community.",
      midNote: 'Bài trung bình (khoảng 90 từ) có hai lý do đúng nhưng chỉ nêu, không giải thích cơ chế (vì sao mua ở cửa hàng nhỏ giúp cộng đồng); "nobody helps you" khái quát quá mức; không có ví dụ. Thang ETS khoảng 3/5. Bài mẫu có ví dụ cá nhân với kết quả cụ thể (hết đau gối), giải thích tác động kinh tế – xã hội và nhượng bộ công bằng.',
      outline: ['Mở bài: bối cảnh + lựa chọn cửa hàng địa phương', 'Thân 1: dịch vụ cá nhân, tư vấn chuyên môn + ví dụ mua giày chạy', 'Thân 2: lợi ích cho cộng đồng – tiền ở lại địa phương, phố xá sống động', 'Nhượng bộ: giá rẻ, lựa chọn rộng, tiện lợi', 'Kết bài'],
      checklist: CK_ESSAY },
  );
